
========================================================================
  PART 1: THE STAIRCASE YOUNG DIAGRAM delta_k
========================================================================

  The STAIRCASE partition delta_k = (k, k-1, ..., 2, 1).
  It has |delta_k| = C(k+1, 2) = k(k+1)/2 boxes.
  Its Ferrers diagram is a right triangle with legs of length k.

  For tournaments on n vertices:
    k = n-2
    |delta_{n-2}| = C(n-1, 2) = number of non-base arcs = m

    n=3: k=1, |delta_1|=1, total arcs=C(3,2)=3, base arcs=2
    n=4: k=2, |delta_2|=3, total arcs=C(4,2)=6, base arcs=3
    n=5: k=3, |delta_3|=6, total arcs=C(5,2)=10, base arcs=4
    n=6: k=4, |delta_4|=10, total arcs=C(6,2)=15, base arcs=5
    n=7: k=5, |delta_5|=15, total arcs=C(7,2)=21, base arcs=6
    n=8: k=6, |delta_6|=21, total arcs=C(8,2)=28, base arcs=7

  KEY INSIGHT: the staircase delta_{n-2} has C(n-1,2) boxes,
  NOT C(n,2). The 'missing' n-1 arcs are the BASE PATH P_0.
  The tiling model fixes P_0 and assigns binary tiles to delta_{n-2}.
  But ALL tournaments live in the FULL hypercube Q_{C(n,2)}.
  The staircase is the REDUCED space after fixing the base path.


========================================================================
  PART 2: HOOK LENGTHS OF THE STAIRCASE
========================================================================

  The HOOK LENGTH h(i,j) = arm + leg + 1.
  For delta_k, the paper proves: ALL hook lengths are ODD.

  delta_1: hooks = [[1]]
    All odd: True, product = 1, f^lambda = 1! / 1 = 1

  delta_2: hooks = [[3, 1], [1]]
    All odd: True, product = 3, f^lambda = 3! / 3 = 2

  delta_3: hooks = [[5, 3, 1], [3, 1], [1]]
    All odd: True, product = 45, f^lambda = 6! / 45 = 16

  delta_4: hooks = [[7, 5, 3, 1], [5, 3, 1], [3, 1], [1]]
    All odd: True, product = 4725, f^lambda = 10! / 4725 = 768

  delta_5: hooks = [[9, 7, 5, 3, 1], [7, 5, 3, 1], [5, 3, 1], [3, 1], [1]]
    All odd: True, product = 4465125, f^lambda = 15! / 4465125 = 292864

  delta_6: hooks = [[11, 9, 7, 5, 3, 1], [9, 7, 5, 3, 1], [7, 5, 3, 1], [5, 3, 1], [3, 1], [1]]
    All odd: True, product = 46414974375, f^lambda = 21! / 46414974375 = 1100742656

  VERIFICATION: All hooks of delta_k are odd for k=1..6. CHECK.

  WHY ARE ALL HOOKS ODD?
  For box (i,j) in delta_k (0-indexed, row i has k-i boxes):
    arm = (k-i) - j - 1
    leg = (k-j) - i - 1  (by symmetry of the staircase)
    h(i,j) = arm + leg + 1 = (k-i-j-1) + (k-j-i-1) + 1
           = 2(k-i-j) - 1
  This is ALWAYS ODD (form 2m-1). QED.

  THE HOOK LENGTH FORMULA:
  h(i,j) = 2(k-i-j) - 1 for box (i,j) in delta_k
  This means hook = 2*diagonal_distance - 1
  where diagonal_distance = k - i - j = distance from the hypotenuse.

  THE DIAGONAL STRUCTURE:
  Boxes on the same anti-diagonal (i+j = constant) have the SAME hook length.
  Anti-diagonal d (where i+j = d) has d+1 boxes (but limited by staircase shape).
  Actually: anti-diagonal d has min(d+1, k-d) boxes.

  delta_1 diagonals (i+j=d): {0: 1}
  delta_2 diagonals (i+j=d): {0: 3, 1: 1}
  delta_3 diagonals (i+j=d): {0: 5, 1: 3, 2: 1}
  delta_4 diagonals (i+j=d): {0: 7, 1: 5, 2: 3, 3: 1}
  delta_5 diagonals (i+j=d): {0: 9, 1: 7, 2: 5, 3: 3, 4: 1}
  delta_6 diagonals (i+j=d): {0: 11, 1: 9, 2: 7, 3: 5, 4: 3, 5: 1}

  Each anti-diagonal has a SINGLE hook value h = 2(k-d)-1.
  The hypotenuse IS the anti-diagonal d = k-1, with hook = 1.
  The corner (0,0) has hook = 2k-1 (the MAXIMUM hook).

========================================================================
  PART 3: STANDARD YOUNG TABLEAUX OF STAIRCASE SHAPE
========================================================================

  f^{delta_k} = number of SYT of shape delta_k.
  By the hook length formula: f^{delta_k} = (C(k+1,2))! / prod(hooks)

  delta_1: 1 boxes, hook_prod = 1, f^delta = 1
  delta_2: 3 boxes, hook_prod = 3, f^delta = 2
  delta_3: 6 boxes, hook_prod = 45, f^delta = 16
  delta_4: 10 boxes, hook_prod = 4725, f^delta = 768
  delta_5: 15 boxes, hook_prod = 4465125, f^delta = 292864
  delta_6: 21 boxes, hook_prod = 46414974375, f^delta = 1100742656
  delta_7: 28 boxes, hook_prod = 6272287562165625, f^delta = 48608795688960

  f^{delta_k} sequence: [1, 2, 16, 768, 292864, 1100742656, 48608795688960]
  This is OEIS A005118: number of SYT of staircase shape.

  COMPARISON WITH TOURNAMENT COUNTS:
    n=3 (k=1): 2^C(n,2) = 8 tournaments, 2^C(n-1,2) = 2 tilings, f^delta_1 = 1
      ratio tilings/f^delta = 2.0000
    n=4 (k=2): 2^C(n,2) = 64 tournaments, 2^C(n-1,2) = 8 tilings, f^delta_2 = 2
      ratio tilings/f^delta = 4.0000
    n=5 (k=3): 2^C(n,2) = 1024 tournaments, 2^C(n-1,2) = 64 tilings, f^delta_3 = 16
      ratio tilings/f^delta = 4.0000
    n=6 (k=4): 2^C(n,2) = 32768 tournaments, 2^C(n-1,2) = 1024 tilings, f^delta_4 = 768
      ratio tilings/f^delta = 1.3333
    n=7 (k=5): 2^C(n,2) = 2097152 tournaments, 2^C(n-1,2) = 32768 tilings, f^delta_5 = 292864
      ratio tilings/f^delta = 0.1119
    n=8 (k=6): 2^C(n,2) = 268435456 tournaments, 2^C(n-1,2) = 2097152 tilings, f^delta_6 = 1100742656
      ratio tilings/f^delta = 0.0019

  The SYT count f^{delta_k} is MUCH SMALLER than 2^m (binary tilings).
  f^delta counts ORDERED fillings; 2^m counts BINARY assignments.
  These are fundamentally different objects -- but related through RSK!


========================================================================
  PART 4: RSK CORRESPONDENCE ON TOURNAMENTS
========================================================================

  RSK maps an n x n matrix to a pair (P, Q) of tableaux.
  For TOURNAMENTS: every tournament has a Hamiltonian path (Redei).
  So the longest chain = n, meaning lambda_1 >= n in any RSK shape.

  But wait -- the tournament A is n x n with C(n,2) ones.
  The RSK of A maps to SEMISTANDARD tableaux, not standard.
  The SHAPE lambda of the RSK output satisfies Greene's theorem:
    lambda_1 + ... + lambda_k = max union of k antichains.

  For tournaments, an ANTICHAIN in the poset sense doesn't apply
  directly since tournaments aren't posets (they have cycles).
  Instead, we work with the LONGEST CHAIN = Hamiltonian path = n.

  THE KEY OBSERVATION:
  The tournament matrix A is ASYMMETRIC: A + A^T = J - I.
  RSK applied to A gives shape lambda. RSK applied to A^T gives lambda'.
  Since A^T = tournament complement T^op, we get:
  RSK(T^op) has shape = CONJUGATE of RSK(T).

  COMPUTING RSK SHAPES FOR n=3 TOURNAMENTS:

  H=1: RSK shapes = {(3,): 2, (2, 1): 4}
  H=3: RSK shapes = {(2, 1): 2}

  n=4 RSK SHAPES:
  H=1 (24 tournaments): top shapes = {(4, 2): 12, (5, 1): 4, (4, 1, 1): 4}
  H=3 (16 tournaments): top shapes = {(4, 2): 8, (4, 1, 1): 4, (3, 2, 1): 4}
  H=5 (24 tournaments): top shapes = {(3, 2, 1): 10, (3, 3): 6, (4, 2): 4}

  n=5 RSK SHAPES (sampling):
  H=1 (120): top shapes = {(6, 3, 1): 44, (5, 4, 1): 36, (5, 3, 2): 10}
  H=3 (120): top shapes = {(5, 4, 1): 38, (6, 3, 1): 32, (5, 3, 2): 14}
  H=5 (240): top shapes = {(5, 3, 2): 68, (6, 3, 1): 56, (5, 4, 1): 40}
  H=9 (240): top shapes = {(5, 4, 1): 56, (5, 3, 2): 50, (6, 3, 1): 32}
  H=11 (120): top shapes = {(5, 3, 2): 26, (5, 4, 1): 20, (5, 3, 1, 1): 16}
  H=13 (120): top shapes = {(6, 3, 1): 20, (5, 4, 1): 18, (5, 3, 2): 14}
  H=15 (64): top shapes = {(5, 4, 1): 20, (5, 3, 2): 10, (5, 3, 1, 1): 8}

========================================================================
  PART 5: TRANSFER MATRIX ON THE HYPOTENUSE
========================================================================

  From kind-pasteur S20p: the staircase delta_k decomposes as:
    delta_k = delta_{k-2} (inner) + L-shaped border (2k-1 tiles)
  The L-border consists of two strips:
    Strip 1 (bottom row): k tiles
    Strip 2 (rightmost column): k-1 tiles
  Total: 2k-1 tiles.

  The HYPOTENUSE INTERFACE between delta_{k-2} and the L-border
  has k-2 tiles. These tiles determine how the inner and outer
  tilings interact.

  For n=5 (k=3): interface has 1 tile -> 2 states
  For n=6 (k=4): interface has 2 tiles -> 4 states
  For n=7 (k=5): interface has 3 tiles -> 8 states

  BUILDING TRANSFER MATRIX FOR n=5:
  delta_3 = (3,2,1), 6 boxes.
  Inner delta_1 = (1), 1 box.
  L-border: 5 boxes. Interface: 1 box.

  The staircase delta_k = (k, k-1, ..., 1) has C(k+1,2) boxes.
  For n vertices, k = n-2, so |delta_{n-2}| = C(n-1,2) boxes.

  Let me compute the actual H values grouped by inner/border structure.
  n=5: 6 tiles, 64 tilings.

  Non-base arcs (sorted by pin position): [(2, 0), (3, 1), (4, 2), (3, 0), (4, 1), (4, 0)]
  Pin positions: [(1, 0), (1, 1), (1, 2), (2, 0), (2, 1), (3, 0)]
  Inner arc: (3,1) at index 1
  Interface arcs: (2,0) at idx 0, (4,2) at idx 2

  H grouped by (inner_bit, interface_bits):
    inner=0, iface=(0, 0): mean H=11.75, H dist={9: 1, 11: 4, 13: 2, 15: 1}
    inner=0, iface=(0, 1): mean H=9.25, H dist={5: 2, 9: 4, 13: 1, 15: 1}
    inner=0, iface=(1, 0): mean H=9.25, H dist={5: 2, 9: 4, 13: 1, 15: 1}
    inner=0, iface=(1, 1): mean H=9.25, H dist={3: 1, 5: 2, 9: 1, 11: 1, 13: 2, 15: 1}
    inner=1, iface=(0, 0): mean H=13.25, H dist={9: 1, 13: 4, 15: 3}
    inner=1, iface=(0, 1): mean H=8.75, H dist={3: 1, 5: 1, 9: 3, 11: 2, 13: 1}
    inner=1, iface=(1, 0): mean H=8.75, H dist={3: 1, 5: 1, 9: 3, 11: 2, 13: 1}
    inner=1, iface=(1, 1): mean H=8.75, H dist={1: 1, 5: 2, 9: 1, 11: 2, 13: 1, 15: 1}

  TRANSFER MATRIX (mean H for each inner x interface state):
  T[inner, iface] = mean H:
          iface=(0,0)  (0,1)  (1,0)  (1,1)
